  "worked_solution": "First, recall that ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}=0,$ means the following: \n\nFor any real $\u03b5>0$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have \n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}|<\u03b5.\n$$\n\nThis can also be expressed as follows:\n\nFor any real $\u03b5>0$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n,$ if $n\\ge N,$ then \n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}|<\u03b5.\n$$\n\nTranslating this into symbolic form, we get\n\n$$\n\u2200\u03b5>0,\\,\u2203N\\in N,\u2200n\\in N,\\,n\\ge N\\,\u21d2\\,|\\frac{1}{(n+3)^{2}}|<\u03b5.\n$$",
